         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p id=\"circtri1\"><style>.circle-draw circle, .circle-draw line {stroke: #71a6d2; stroke-width: 4; stroke-linecap: round; fill: none;} .circle-draw circle { stroke-dasharray: 942; stroke-dashoffset: 942; animation: drawCircle 2s linear forwards;} .radius-line { transform-origin: 300px 170px; animation: rotateRadius 2s linear forwards;} .radius-45 { opacity: 0; animation: showRadius45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .radius-30{ opacity: 0; animation: showRadius30 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .vertical45 { opacity: 0; animation: showVertical45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.5s; } .atlasTextRadius { opacity: 0; animation: atlasTextRadiusMov 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.8s; }</style><style>@keyframes drawCircle { to {stroke-dashoffset: 0; } } @keyframes rotateRadius { from { transform: rotate(0deg); } to   { transform: rotate(-360deg); } } @keyframes showRadius45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showRadius30 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showVertical45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }  @keyframes atlasTextRadiusMov { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }</style><div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 -70 600 410\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 320 147 A 30 30 0 0 1 340.0 170.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"520\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"-55\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"170\" r=\"148\" transform=\"scale(-1 1) translate(-600 0)  rotate(180 300 170)\"></circle> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"-41\"></line> <line class=\"vertical45\" x1=\"405\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"-41\"></line> <line class=\"vertical45\" x1=\"405\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"169\"></line> <line class=\"radius-45\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"405\" y2=\"64\"></line> <line class=\"vertical45\" x1=\"405\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"405\" y2=\"166\"></line> <line class=\"vertical45\" x1=\"405\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"301\" y2=\"169\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"95\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">1</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"208\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">csc θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"365\" y=\"15\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">cot θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"470\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">tan θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"345\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">cos θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"419\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" transform=\"rotate(90, 419, 115)\">sin θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"320\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"1.0em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"-58\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">y</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"396\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"520\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">x</text></svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 1</b>: The circle with radius 1 and the trigonometric segments built on it: cos θ, sin θ, tan θ, cot θ and csc θ.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### The other opposite side\n\n**Graph 1** shows the trigonometric relationships on the circle. Besides the right triangle whose hypotenuse is the radius 1, it contains the cosecant: the segment from the center O up the y axis, ending where the tangent at **P** meets that axis. We will focus on that segment and predict which values csc(θ) can take in the first quadrant we are studying.\n\n#### Isolating\n\nTo make this easier to see, we will isolate from **Graph 1** only the parts this study needs:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 -70 600 270\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker></defs>  <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 230 169 A 30 30 0 0 0 221.2 147.8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"-41\"></line> <line class=\"vertical45\" x1=\"305\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"-41\"></line> <line class=\"radius-45\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"305\" y2=\"64\"></line>  <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"182\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">1</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"182\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"108\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">csc θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"265\" y=\"15\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">cot θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"218\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"1.0em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"182\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"298\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text> </svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 2</b>: The triangle from <b>Graph 1</b> isolated: there the cosecant is the hypotenuse.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The two points P\n\nIn **Graph 2**, two points are both named **P**, and both sit at the same distance from the center O — the radius of the circle. The **P** on the circle marks the position of the angle θ, while the **P** on the vertical axis marks that same radius length carried up the y axis. Naming them alike reminds us that the two points represent one and the same radius R of the circle; the vertical line then continues past the upper **P** to the end of the cosecant segment.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The definition\n\nThe segment we see in **Graph 2** also received a mathematical definition in an earlier text: while manipulating trigonometric relationships, we worked with the cosecant as the reciprocal of the sine (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f74e0412-ac03-4bc4-b6a1-fa22dd5c441d",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Playing with the Cotangent",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>csc (θ)</mi><mn>=</mn><mfrac><mi>1</mi><mi>sin (θ)</mi></mfrac></math></p> <p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Definition of the cosecant as the reciprocal of the sine.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Curiosity\n\nA point made by Eric W. Weisstein deserves mention: this function originated almost alongside the sine, but its use declined so much that it came to be counted among the rarely used functions (<htcite0>).\n\n#### Graph\n\nWe can predict the graph of the cosecant by comparing it with the cotangent, because both functions divide by sin(θ). We first learned in an earlier text that the cotangent is the cosine divided by the sine (<htcite1>); that relationship is our **Formula 2**:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ce379e76-6a55-4e10-985b-a29f4d65cee3",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Cosecant",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f74e0412-ac03-4bc4-b6a1-fa22dd5c441d",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Playing with the Cotangent",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>cot (θ)</mi><mn>=</mn><mfrac><mi>cos(θ)</mi><mi>sin (θ)</mi></mfrac></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 2</b>: Definition of the cotangent in terms of the sine and the cosine.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Comparing **Formulas 1 and 2**, we see that both divide by sin(θ); only the numerator changes: **Formula 1** divides 1 by sin(θ), while **Formula 2** divides cos(θ) by sin(θ). From this we can predict the graph of the cosecant:\n- When sin(θ) reaches its greatest value, 1, csc(θ) is also 1 — dividing 1 by 1 changes nothing;\n- As another *History Tracers* text notes, infinitely many numbers lie between 0 and 1 (<htcite0>). When sin(θ) shrinks from 1 toward 0, dividing 1 by a smaller and smaller positive number makes csc(θ) grow;\n- When sin(θ) = 0, the division is undefined, so csc(θ) has no value there.\n\nTo see this in a picture, look at **Graph 3**:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d06e24cb-52e8-4ff8-ae37-12052c91b187",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Limiting",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chartcsc1\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 3</b>: Graph of the cosecant function in the first quadrant: it comes down from very large values and reaches 1 at θ = 90°. Graph created with the <htcite0> library.</div></p><script> htPlotCosecant('chartcsc1', 'theta (radians)', 'cosecant (θ)', 1);</script>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To make the comparison with the cotangent easier, we draw it below:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htChart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chartcot2\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 4</b>: Graph of the cotangent function, drawn here for comparison. Graph created with the <htcite0> library.</div></p><script> htPlotCotangent('chartcot2', 'cotangent (θ)', 1);</script>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6efe7632-faed-4080-a5aa-38463d43e6d7",
                     "text": "Chart JS",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### From one graph to the other\n\n**Graph 1** shows the cosecant in an intermediate position. We will now move the radius to the two extremes so we can understand the values that appear in **Graph 3**.\n\nWe start with the case where sin(θ) reaches its greatest value and, as a consequence, csc(θ) reaches its smallest:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p id=\"circtri3\"><style>.circle-draw circle, .circle-draw line {stroke: #71a6d2; stroke-width: 4; stroke-linecap: round; fill: none;} .circle-draw circle { stroke-dasharray: 942; stroke-dashoffset: 942; animation: drawCircle 2s linear forwards;} .radius-line { transform-origin: 300px 170px; animation: rotateRadius 2s linear forwards;} .radius-45 { opacity: 0; animation: showRadius45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .radius-30{ opacity: 0; animation: showRadius30 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .vertical45 { opacity: 0; animation: showVertical45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.5s; } .atlasTextRadius { opacity: 0; animation: atlasTextRadiusMov 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.8s; }</style><style>@keyframes drawCircle { to {stroke-dashoffset: 0; } } @keyframes rotateRadius { from { transform: rotate(0deg); } to   { transform: rotate(-360deg); } } @keyframes showRadius45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showRadius30 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showVertical45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }  @keyframes atlasTextRadiusMov { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }</style><div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 340\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\"> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"520\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"8\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"170\" r=\"148\" transform=\"scale(-1 1) translate(-600 0)  rotate(180 300 170)\"></circle> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"21\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"285\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">1</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"308\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">csc θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"308\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">sin θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"9\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">y</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"295\" y=\"15\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"520\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">x</text></svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 5</b>: Starting position: the radius points up the y axis, so sin θ = 1 and csc θ = 1.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "As the radius begins to rotate downward, sin(θ) decreases toward zero. As a consequence, csc(θ) grows without bound. In the limit where sin(θ) approaches zero, the figure would look like **Graph 6**:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p id=\"circtri4\"><style>.circle-draw circle, .circle-draw line {stroke: #71a6d2; stroke-width: 4; stroke-linecap: round; fill: none;} .circle-draw circle { stroke-dasharray: 942; stroke-dashoffset: 942; animation: drawCircle 2s linear forwards;} .radius-line { transform-origin: 300px 170px; animation: rotateRadius 2s linear forwards;} .radius-45 { opacity: 0; animation: showRadius45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .radius-30{ opacity: 0; animation: showRadius30 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .vertical45 { opacity: 0; animation: showVertical45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.5s; } .atlasTextRadius { opacity: 0; animation: atlasTextRadiusMov 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.8s; }</style><style>@keyframes drawCircle { to {stroke-dashoffset: 0; } } @keyframes rotateRadius { from { transform: rotate(0deg); } to   { transform: rotate(-360deg); } } @keyframes showRadius45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showRadius30 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showVertical45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }  @keyframes atlasTextRadiusMov { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }</style><div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 340\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\"> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"520\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"8\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"170\" r=\"148\" transform=\"scale(-1 1) translate(-600 0)  rotate(180 300 170)\"></circle>  <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"445\" y2=\"150\"></line> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"-200\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">1</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"325\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">sin θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"9\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">y</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"450\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"425\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">Q</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"520\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">x</text></svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 6</b>: Limit case: as the radius falls toward the x axis, sin θ approaches 0 and the cosecant grows so long that its label leaves the figure.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In this last graph we kept sin(θ) visible, but the cosecant has stretched so far that its label no longer fits in the figure.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Practicing\n\nNow let us practice what we just learned. In **Graph 5**, the radius pointed up the y axis, where sin(θ) has its greatest value, 1, and — as a consequence — csc(θ) has its smallest value, also 1. That is exactly the starting position of the interactive app in **Graph 7**.\n\nClick on the chart to move the radius through the first quadrant. Watch the right triangle and θ change: as the radius falls away from the vertical, sin(θ) shrinks while csc(θ) grows — just as **Graph 3** predicted and **Graph 6** illustrated. Try several positions and guess sin and csc before reading them in the panel under the chart.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
